<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Kyrgyzstan</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Kyrgyz Republic, Kirghizia</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Kyrgyzstan, officially the Kyrgyz Republic and formerly Kirghizia, is a landlocked country in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-asia">Central Asia</a>, lying in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tian-shan">Tian Shan</a> and Pamir mountain ranges. Its lands were part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silk-road">Silk Road</a>, and it was first established as the Yenisei Kyrgyz Khaganate. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongol-empire">Mongol Empire</a> conquered it in the 13th century, and later the Dzungar Khanate invaded; Kyrgyz and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kipchaks">Kipchaks</a> then became part of the Kokand Khanate. In 1876 Kyrgyzstan became part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russian-empire">Russian Empire</a>, and in 1936 the Kirghiz Soviet Socialist Republic was formed as a constituent republic of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/askar-akayev">Askar Akayev</a> was elected president in 1990, and Kyrgyzstan declared independence on 31 August 1991.</p></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>…1991 and the United Nations on 2 March 1992, and belongs to the OSCE, the Organisation of Islamic Cooperation, the Organisation of Turkic States and the Shanghai Cooperation Organisation; it joined GUAM in 1999 and left in 2005, and founded the Central Asian Union with Kazakhstan and <b>Kyrgyzstan</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/uzbekistan">Uzbekistan · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>…as one of the world’s oldest religions, and since the dissolution of the Soviet Union it has spread among intellectuals in Kazakhstan, <b>Kyrgyzstan</b>, Tatarstan and Bashkortostan and is undergoing an organised revival in Siberia; Nursultan Nazarbayev and Askar Akayev called it the “natural” religion of the Turkic peoples.</blockquote><a href="/religion/tengrism">Tengrism · Legacy</a></li><li><blockquote>The Orkhon inscriptions, which stand in the Orkhon valley of Mongolia, whose cultural landscape is a World Heritage Site, are revered as national monuments in Turkey, Kazakhstan, <b>Kyrgyzstan</b> and other Turkic countries, and their words are read in schools.</blockquote><a href="/language/old-turkic">Old Turkic · Legacy</a></li><li><blockquote>The names of modern assemblies, Mongolia’s State Great Khural and the kurultais of Kazakhstan, <b>Kyrgyzstan</b> and Bashkortostan, recall the old assembly, and Mongolia’s Naadam festival keeps the wrestling, horse racing and archery that accompanied it.</blockquote><a href="/government/nomadic-confederation">Nomadic confederation · Impact &amp; Legacy</a></li></ul></section>
